<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acoma Pueblo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acoma Pueblo is a Native American pueblo built on a sandstone mesa west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, and is among the oldest continuously inhabited settlements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. After its people killed a party of Spanish soldiers in 1598, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a>'s forces stormed the mesa in January 1599, killed several hundred inhabitants and sentenced the survivors to servitude and mutilation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> was later tried and punished for his conduct.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo.jpg" alt="Acoma Pueblo" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scott Catron · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acoma_Pueblo_Sky_City_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Acoma is a Keres pueblo whose old village, Sky City, stands on a mesa 365 feet high, about 60 miles west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a>; with Acomita, Anzac and McCartys it makes up the village of Acoma Pueblo. Its people descend from the Ancestral Puebloans, the Mogollon and other earlier peoples. The pueblo emerged by the 13th century, though the Acoma themselves date Sky City to the 11th century. The site's isolation sheltered the community from raids by neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> peoples. The name comes, through Spanish, from the Acoma word for a person from Acoma Pueblo; it does not mean sky city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Estevanico, an enslaved man of Moroccan birth, was the first outsider known to visit, in 1539. In 1540 Hernando de Alvarado of Francisco Vázquez de Coronado's expedition described the place as one of the strongest he had seen, reached only by a hand-built stairway and holes cut in the rock. Later Spanish parties came in 1581 and 1582. In December 1598 the Acoma killed Juan de Zaldívar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a>'s nephew, and eleven of his men; Acoma oral tradition says the fighting began when the soldiers attacked Acoma women. In the assault of January 1599 led by Vicente de Zaldívar, the Spanish burned most of the village, cut off the right foot of men over 25 and forced survivors into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> for 20 years.</p><p>Survivors rebuilt the town between 1599 and 1620. Between 1629 and 1641 the Acoma were made to build the San Esteban del Rey Mission, hauling about 20,000 tons of adobe, stone and mud up the mesa and pine beams from Mount Taylor, more than 40 miles away. The Acoma took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680, kept their own religion in secret beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> and Ute. Railroads ended their isolation in the 1880s, and by 1922 most children had been sent to boarding schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 300 two- and three-storey adobe buildings stand on the mesa, which has no electricity, running water or sewage system; a few dozen people live there permanently. The pueblo is a National Historic Landmark, and the Acoma hold a reservation of about 500,000 acres of their traditional land. Each September the feast of Saint Stephen opens the mesa to the public, and Acoma pottery is made in a tradition more than 1,000 years old.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acoma Pueblo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His brutal punishment of <b>Ácoma Pueblo</b> in 1599 led to his recall.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
